{"id": "q12", "claim": "c3", "kind": "claim", "form": "change-condition", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s17", "s18"], "question": "The paper's ablation index reports a sweep of pull strength for its target-side pull-toward-reconstruction objective. Over what range was the strength varied, and with what outcome? Suppose an auditor tries to recover the lost slot content afterward using the paper's label-free realignment, the procedure that repairs probe drift. Would it work? Base your answer on the paper's evidence that separates collapse from drift.", "answer": "Pull strength was varied over λ = 0–10, and collapse occurred at every setting, so no strength avoids it. Realignment would not recover the content. In drift, the information persists and an affine map restores native probe performance in 9/9 cases. After collapse, freshly retrained probes also lose accuracy, so the information itself is gone and there is nothing for a realignment map to recover.", "rubric": ["States the tested range was λ = 0 to 10 and that collapse occurred at every strength.", "Concludes that realignment would not recover the content.", "Reasons from the contrast: after collapse even freshly retrained probes fail, so the information is gone; in drift the information persisted and affine realignment restored it."]},
{"id": "q14", "claim": "c4", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3", "s5", "s14"], "question": "The paper distinguishes a storage gap from a readout gap. A proposed variant keeps the target model's weights frozen and adds RECAP's linear heads, with the same external targets, to the reader-side objective (the paper's Eq. 1 setting) instead of Eq. 3. Which gap does the paper assign to the released Qwen-2.5-7B NLA's failure, and what decodability figure supports that diagnosis? Why does this diagnosis rule out the variant?", "answer": "The paper attributes the Qwen failure to a storage gap. Input specifics decode from the activation at only about 6% chance-corrected, and this figure is flat across layers 16–27. The content is therefore largely absent from the activation. The variant optimizes only within Eq. 1, which never updates θ, so it cannot change what the activation stores. Only Eq. 3, which trains θ, can add the content.", "rubric": ["Identifies the Qwen failure as a storage gap (specifics largely absent from the activation).", "Cites the roughly 6% chance-corrected decodability of specifics, unchanged across layers.", "Explains that a reader-side or frozen-θ objective (Eq. 1) cannot change what the representation contains, whereas RECAP's Eq. 3 trains θ."]},
{"id": "q16", "claim": "c4", "kind": "claim", "form": "change-condition", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3", "s5", "s13", "s20"], "question": "A skeptic argues that the near-perfect truth of fresh sandbox NLAs on RECAP target models comes from their supervised warm-up on gold descriptions, not from RECAP. Using the paper's training recipe for the toy NLAs and its audit results for NLAs on standard target models, explain why the paper's data contradict this.", "answer": "The NLAs on standard target models received the same cold-start SFT on gold descriptions and identical subsequent training. Yet they state partially decodable slots truly only about 0.15–0.26 of the time, and they develop private codes in 5/5 runs. If the SFT produced truthfulness, the standard-model NLAs would be truthful too. Since only the RECAP-side NLAs reach about 1.00 truth, the difference must come from the target model's decodability.", "rubric": ["Notes that NLAs on both RECAP and standard target models received the same cold-start SFT on gold descriptions, with identical training otherwise.", "Cites that standard-model NLAs nonetheless have low truth on present slots (e.g., 0.15–0.26) and/or develop codes in every standard run.", "Concludes that SFT cannot explain the gap, so the gain is attributable to RECAP's target-model decodability."]},
{"id": "q17", "claim": "c5", "kind": "claim", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s5", "s20"], "question": "Two designated targets in the paper's Pythia-160M RECAP runs have estimated window base rates of 2% and 0.5%. Under the paper's class-balanced presence loss as specified in its reproducibility notes, what positive-example weight does each receive? What failure at the paper's largest target count is this weighting meant to fix, and how did the paper detect that failure?", "answer": "The weights are 50 and 100. The paper uses the inverse base rate, 1/0.02 = 50, and 1/0.005 = 200 is clamped at 100. At K=512 the naive head minimized its training loss by always predicting 'absent' for rare targets. This failure was visible only because an independent fresh probe found nothing decodable. Balancing makes the constant prediction costly and restored 0.97–0.99 AUC.", "rubric": ["Gives weights of 50 and 100, with the second reflecting the clamp at 100 on an inverse base rate of 200.", "States that the naive K=512 head minimized its loss by always predicting absent.", "Notes that the failure was exposed by an independent fresh probe (head loss looked fine), and/or that balancing restored high AUC."]},
{"id": "q27", "claim": "c4", "kind": "integration", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s8", "s14"], "question": "A team proposes to recover the specifics the released Qwen-2.5-7B NLA misses without retraining Qwen. They would read the model's last layer, either with a fitted linear probe or with the model's own logit lens. Using the paper's late-layer inventory of missed values and its stated limitations of RECAP, what share of missed values would each readout surface at the last layer? Why would fitting RECAP-style heads on the frozen model not do better?", "answer": "The logit lens surfaces about 10% of missed true values in the top-50 at layer 27, up from 0.6% at the read layer. The matched ridge probe stays flat at about 5–6%. Roughly 90% of missed specifics therefore remain unrecovered. RECAP must be co-trained into θ and cannot be retrofitted onto a frozen model, so linear heads fitted on frozen weights are just linear probes like the flat ridge readout.", "rubric": ["States that the logit lens recovers at most about 10% (top-50 at layer 27) while the linear/ridge probe stays flat around 5–6%, leaving most missed specifics unrecovered.", "Explains that RECAP requires co-training the target model and cannot be retrofitted; heads on frozen weights amount to probes and add no content."]}
]}
